syntax:
C++ syntax for implicit conversions involves conversion operators or constructors:

class X {
public:
    operator Y() const;  // Conversion operator from X to Y
};

class Y {
public:
    Y(const X& x);  // Implicit constructor from X to Y
    bool operator==(const Y& other) const;  // Equality operator
};

Implementation notes: The compiler checks for exact matches first, then user-defined conversions, and finally standard conversions. For the expression 'yy.operator==(xx.getValue())', if getValue() returns X and operator== expects Y, the conversion operator or constructor is invoked, creating a temporary Y object with its own memory layout, including any vtable if Y is polymorphic.
